npu-llm — LLM on the QCS6490 Hexagon NPU (V68)
==============================================

What this is
------------
Two LLMs pre-compiled as QAIRT context binaries for Hexagon V68 (QCS6490),
plus the genie-t2t-run runtime and the QNN HTP V68 libraries they need.

  /usr/bin/npu-llm                      CLI launcher
  /usr/bin/npu-chat                     fullscreen GTK4 chat UI
  /usr/lib/npu-llm/                     genie-t2t-run + libGenie/libQnnHtp (private)
  /usr/lib/npu-llm/voice/               sherpa-onnx python bindings (private)
  /usr/share/npu-llm/qwen2.5-0.5b/      Qwen2.5-0.5B, W4, 1024 ctx
  /usr/share/npu-llm/llama3.2-1b/       Llama-3.2-1B, W4, 4096 ctx
  /usr/share/npu-llm/voice/{asr,tts}/   SenseVoice ASR + VITS TTS models
  /usr/share/applications/npu-chat.desktop

Both run entirely on the NPU (QnnHtp backend, cdsp domain).

Usage
-----
  npu-chat                              # fullscreen chat, no window chrome
  npu-llm list
  npu-llm qwen2.5-0.5b -i               # interactive chat on stdin/stdout
  npu-llm qwen2.5-0.5b -p "Write three sentences about the sea."
  npu-llm llama3.2-1b  -p "hi" --profile /tmp/prof.json

No GUI needed: -i gives a REPL on any terminal (serial console, ssh, adb
shell). Type a line, press Enter, get the answer; Ctrl-D or "exit" quits. It
keeps the last 6 turns in the prompt. Every turn reloads the context binary
(~1.2 s) and re-prefills the history, because genie-t2t-run has no session
model — expect ~2 s overhead on top of generation.

Over adb from a host:

  ./quectel_build/tools/adb -s <serial> shell npu-llm qwen2.5-0.5b -i

qwen2.5-0.5b is the faster one and much better at Chinese; llama3.2-1b is more
coherent in English. Neither keeps state between runs of the one-shot -p form.

The GUI needs a Wayland or X session (log in on the HDMI output — tty1 shows
the GDM greeter; pick user `p`). It is undecorated and fullscreen by design;
Esc quits. To start it automatically at login, copy the .desktop file:

  cp /usr/share/applications/npu-chat.desktop /etc/xdg/autostart/

Sampling: the Llama bundle shipped greedy sampling (top-k 1) and looped until
it hit the 512-token cap (65 s of repeated lines on the poem prompt). This
package sets top-k 40, the same as the Qwen bundle: the same prompt now ends at
EOS in 6 s. Edit /usr/share/npu-llm/<model>/model-config.json to taste
(temp, top-k, top-p, max-num-tokens).

One process runs per question: ~1.2 s to load the context binary, then text
streams into the UI as genie-t2t-run emits it (the launcher runs it under
stdbuf so nothing is held back by pipe buffering). Stop interrupts generation
with SIGINT and falls back to SIGKILL after 2 s. If the GUI is killed
mid-answer the child is killed with it (PR_SET_PDEATHSIG) — a surviving
orphan keeps the DSP busy and the next run fails with
"Failed to create device: 14001" until reboot.

Measured on a Quectel PI H1 (QCS6490, 8x A55, 8 GB):

  model            prefill      decode
  qwen2.5-0.5b     160 tok/s    20.3 tok/s
  llama3.2-1b      48.6 tok/s    7.9 tok/s

The GUI defaults to llama3.2-1b (more coherent in Chinese and English); switch
models with the chip in the top right corner.

Voice: talk to it, and it talks back
------------------------------------
Everything is offline and on the CPU, so speech keeps working while the NPU
streams the answer:

  ASR  SenseVoice small (int8), zh/en/ja/ko/yue, ~17x real time
  TTS  VITS fanchen-C (zh), 4 threads, ~1.4x real time

In the GUI: press "mic" to start recording, press it again to stop. The
recognition result goes into the entry as if you had typed it, then the normal
send path runs. "speak" (on by default) reads the answer out loud sentence by
sentence as it streams in, so the first sentence is audible ~2 s after the
question. "stop" silences it too.

Recording and playback go through PipeWire (pw-record / pw-play), not ALSA:
this image has no alsa-utils and arecord does not exist. Both need a running
user session — from a root shell:

  systemd-run --uid=p --scope --setenv=XDG_RUNTIME_DIR=/run/user/1001 \
      npu-chat

Headless check of the whole chain (record 4 s, transcribe, speak):

  NPU_LLM_VOICE=/usr/share/npu-llm/voice npu-chat --voice-selftest

The TTS lexicon is Chinese only, so the text is normalised before synthesis:
arabic digits become Chinese numerals ("25" -> 二十五, "12" -> 十二, years
digit by digit), 60% -> 百分之六十, and anything the lexicon cannot pronounce
(ASCII letters, emoji, markup) is dropped instead of failing the sentence.

Measured on the same board: 5.6 s of speech recognised in 0.33 s (17x real
time), a 3.9 s sentence synthesised in 2.4 s (1.4x real time) — well inside
the ~20 tok/s the NPU produces.

识图: ask qwen3.5 about a picture
--------------------------------
qwen3.5-0.8b (shipped by the qwen35-npu package, resident on the DSP) carries
a vision tower; the two models in this package do not, so the 识图 button is
disabled while one of them is selected.

  🖼 识图 → 上传图片…     pick a PNG / JPEG / WebP file
  🖼 识图 → 摄像头拍照      grab one frame from /dev/video10

The picture is queued above the entry with a thumbnail. Press send to ask
something about it, or leave the entry empty and the model describes it. One
image goes out per request as a base64 data URL — the server takes no remote
URLs and caps the decoded image at 8 MB — and only on the turn it was picked
for: replaying it later would add ~650 prompt tokens (≈9 s of prefill) while
the answer already describes what was in it.

The camera is the CamxBridge v4l2loopback node (1280x720), the same one
qcom-npu-test uses; only one process can hold it. The bridge emits a black
frame or two while it builds the stream, so a capture reads a few frames and
keeps the last non-dark one. If it never produces an image:

  systemctl restart camx-v4l2-bridge@0

A picture costs ~1.3 s in the vision encoder plus ~650 tokens of prefill, so
its first token lands 11-14 s out; decoding afterwards is unchanged. Headless
check of the same path:

  npu-chat --image /tmp/frame.jpg --ask "这张图片里有什么？" --shot /tmp/s.png

Why not GGUF on the NPU
-----------------------
ggml-hexagon (llama.cpp) ships backends for HTP v73/v75/v79/v81 only. On a
V68 part it caps the arch to v73 and fails at session setup:

  ggml-hex: Hexagon arch v68 is under supported range, capping at v73
  ggml-hex: dspqueue_read failed: 0x0000002e

So GGUF models (including Qwen3.5) are CPU-only here. The NPU path requires a
context binary compiled for V68, which is what this package ships.

Qwen3.5 in particular cannot be compiled for the NPU: it is a hybrid
architecture (qwen3_5: 3x linear_attention + 1x full_attention per block) and
libQnnGenAiTransformer only supports GPT2/GPTJ/LLAMA/QWEN graphs.

Provenance
----------
Binaries and weights come from the radxa ModelScope bundles
(radxa/Qwen2.5-0.5B-v68, radxa/Llama3.2-1B-4096-qairt-v68), built with
Qualcomm AI Runtime 2.40.1. They are third-party Qualcomm redistributables:
check your licence before shipping them in a product image.

Adding a model
--------------
A bundle needs: model-config.json (dialog JSON), tokenizer.json,
htp_backend_ext_config.json and models/*.bin, all in one directory, plus the
V68 runtime libraries (shared, already in /usr/lib/npu-llm). Prebuilt V68
bundles are scarce — Qualcomm AI Hub publishes V73+ only for current models,
so anything else has to be compiled with QAIRT for V68 yourself.
